**Example:**
```cpp
#include <type_traits>

// Trait template to detect if a type is integral
template <typename T, typename Enable = void>
struct IsIntegral : std::false_type {};

// Specialization for integral types using SFINAE
template <typename T>
struct IsIntegral<T, typename std::enable_if<std::is_integral<T>::value>::type> : std::true_type {};

// Function enabled only for integral types
template <typename T>
auto process(T t) -> typename std::enable_if<IsIntegral<T>::value, void> {
    // Implementation for integral types
    std::cout << t << " is an integral type." << std::endl;
}

// Function enabled only for non-integral types
template <typename T>
auto process(T t) -> typename std::enable_if<!IsIntegral<T>::value, void> {
    // Implementation for non-integral types
    std::cout << t << " is not an integral type." << std::endl;
}

int main() {
    process(42);        // Calls integral version
    process(3.14);      // Calls non-integral version
    process("hello"); // Calls non-integral version
    return 0;
}
```

This example demonstrates how template specialization combined with SFINAE enables conditional compilation of functions based on type traits. The IsIntegral template uses std::enable_if to selectively enable function overloads for integral and non-integral types. When 'process' is called with an integer, the integral version is instantiated and executed; for floating-point or string types, the non-integral version is selected. This approach allows writing flexible, type-safe code that adapts to different types at compile time without runtime overhead, exemplifying advanced template programming techniques in modern C++.

