**Syntax:**
template <class P>
iterator insert(P&& x)  // SFINAE constraint: This overload participates only if std::is_constructible<value_type, P&&>::value is true.
{
    return emplace(std::forward<P>(x));  // Perfect forwarding preserves the value category of x for efficient construction.
}

template <class P>
iterator insert(const_iterator position, P&& x)  // SFINAE constraint: Same as above.
{
    return emplace_hint(position, std::forward<P>(x));  // Uses the hint for potential optimization in tree insertion.
}

**Example:**
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <map>
#include <type_traits>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::multimap<int, std::string> mmap;
    std::string str = "Hello";  // lvalue
    auto it1 = mmap.insert(str);  // Should succeed if std::string is constructible from std::string&&

    // Attempt with incompatible type (e.g., int to std::string, which fails SFINAE)
    // The following line won't compile due to SFINAE: mmap.insert(42);  // Fails because int&& can't construct std::string
}
// Note: Uncommenting the failing line will cause a compilation error, demonstrating SFINAE.
```

In this code, the compiler first evaluates the SFINAE condition for std::multimap::insert during overload resolution. For mmap.insert(str), std::is_constructible<std::string, std::string&&>::value is true, so the template is instantiated, and std::forward<std::string>(str) forwards the lvalue as an lvalue reference, invoking emplace to construct the node efficiently without copying if possible. This results in assembly that might include a move or copy operation based on the argument's category, optimized by the compiler's inlining of std::forward. If we attempted mmap.insert(42), the SFINAE check fails because std::is_constructible<std::string, int&&>::value is false, so this overload is removed from the candidate set, leading to a compile-time error rather than runtime failure. This compile-time behavior ensures that only valid insertions are considered, improving performance by avoiding runtime type checks and directly influencing the generated binary's size and efficiency.

