    volatile int elem = 5;  // Using volatile to prevent aggressive optimizations
    elem = elem * elem;  // Squaring elem; potential for undefined behavior if elem had side effects
    std::cout << "Result: " << elem << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

In this code, the line 'elem = elem * elem;' demonstrates the order of evaluation. The compiler might evaluate the two 'elem' operands in any order (e.g., left first or right first), leading to assembly like: loading elem into EAX, multiplying by elem in EBX (on x86), then storing back. If compiled with g++ -O2, the compiler could optimize by reusing registers, potentially generating: MOV EAX, [elem]; IMUL EAX, EAX; MOV [elem], EAX; This avoids extra memory loads for performance. However, if elem were modified in a function call (not the case here), it could cause undefined behavior due to unspecified evaluation order. Memory-wise, no additional temporaries are needed for this simple expression, keeping stack usage low. At runtime, the multiplication executes dynamically, but if elem were a constant, the compiler might evaluate it at compile-time, illustrating the runtime vs. compile-time dichotomy.

In modern C++, std::fill and std::fill_n are algorithms used to assign a specific value to a range of elements within a container or sequence. They are essential for efficiently initializing or resetting elements in containers such as vectors, arrays, or other iterators. These functions promote clear, concise, and expressive code by abstracting manual loops, and they are part of the Standard Template Library (STL), leveraging iterator concepts for flexibility and generic programming.

**Syntax:**
template <class ForwardIterator, class T>
void fill(ForwardIterator first, ForwardIterator last, const T& value);

template <class OutputIterator, class Size, class T>
OutputIterator fill_n(OutputIterator first, Size n, const T& value);

**Example:**
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> data(10);
    // Fill entire vector with 5
    std::fill(data.begin(), data.end(), 5);
    // Fill first 3 elements with 10
    std::fill_n(data.begin(), 3, 10);
    // Output the vector elements
    for (const auto& elem : data) {
        std::cout << elem << ' ';
    }
    return 0;
}
```

This example demonstrates the use of std::fill to assign the value 5 to all elements in the vector, effectively initializing or resetting the container. Then, std::fill_n assigns the value 10 to the first three elements of the vector. The code showcases how these algorithms simplify bulk value assignment over ranges, leveraging iterators for flexibility and adhering to modern C++ best practices.

