int fact(int n);  // Takes an int and returns an int

int main() {
    int number = 5;  // Input value
    int result = fact(number);  // Calling the function with an argument
    std::cout << "Factorial of " << number << " is " << result << std::endl;
    return 0;
}

// Function definition
int fact(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return 1;  // Base case
    return n * fact(n - 1);  // Recursive call
}
```

This example demonstrates a practical implementation of the factorial function, which is commonly used in real-world applications like combinatorics or probability calculations. By calling fact(5), we supply an integer argument, and the function returns the computed result, showcasing how functions encapsulate logic for reusability. Best practices here include using recursion with a base case to avoid infinite loops—a common pitfall in recursive functions—and ensuring the function handles edge cases (like n <= 1). In real-world usage, such as in data analysis tools, this pattern promotes idiomatic C++ by keeping functions small and focused, improving code maintainability. Additionally, passing by value (int n) is simple here, but for larger data, switching to const references would optimize performance, highlighting the need to choose the right parameter passing strategy based on context.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <type_traits>
template <typename T, typename U = void>
struct is_same_type {
  static constexpr bool value = false;
};
template <typename T>
struct is_same_type<T, T> {
  static constexpr bool value = true;
};
int main() {
  static_assert(is_same_type<int, int>::value, "Error: int is not equal to int");
  static_assert(!is_same_type<int, double>::value, "Error: int is equal to double");
  std::cout << std::is_same_v<int, int> << std::endl; // Using std::is_same_v
  return 0;
}
template<typename T, size_t N> 
struct StaticArray { 
T arr[N]; 
};
int main() {
