    }

    return 0;
}
```

This code example illustrates the practical use of vector initialization in a real-world context, such as preparing a data array for input processing in applications like simulations or data analysis. By initializing std::vector<int> v2(10), we create a collection ready for immediate use, avoiding manual memory allocation errors common with raw arrays. Best practices here include using a range-based for loop for safer iteration in modern C++ (though a traditional loop is shown for clarity), and always checking the size with v2.size() to prevent out-of-bounds access. A common pitfall is modifying the vector without considering reallocation overhead; in performance-critical code, use reserve() first if the size is approximate. This pattern promotes idiomatic C++ by leveraging STL containers for robustness and readability.

