## WHAT

**Prefix increment (`++i`)**: Increment first, then return the new value. Returns reference to the incremented variable.
**Postfix increment (`i++`)**: Return current value first, then increment. Returns a copy of the original value (before increment).

## WHY

- **Prefix (`++i`)**: Use when you don't need the old value. **Faster** for complex types (no temporary copy). Preferred in loops.
- **Postfix (`i++`)**: Use only when you explicitly need the value before increment (e.g., `arr[i++]`).
- **Performance**: For built-in types (int, pointer), compilers optimize both equally. For user-defined types (iterators, custom classes), prefix is faster.
- **Best practice**: Default to `++i` in loops, use `i++` only when semantics require it.

## HOW

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int x = 5;
    int y = 5;

    // Prefix: increment first, then use value
    int a = ++x;  // x becomes 6, a = 6
    cout << "Prefix: x = " << x << ", a = " << a << endl;
    // Output: x = 6, a = 6

    // Postfix: use value first, then increment
    int b = y++;  // b = 5 (old value), then y becomes 6
    cout << "Postfix: y = " << y << ", b = " << b << endl;
    // Output: y = 6, b = 5

    // Common loop idiom
    for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) {  // Prefer ++i (no difference for int, but good habit)
        cout << i << " ";
    }
    cout << endl;

    // Using in array indexing
    int arr[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
    int index = 0;

    cout << arr[index++] << endl;  // Prints 10, then index becomes 1
    cout << arr[++index] << endl;  // index becomes 2, prints 30

    // Return value difference
    int m = 10;
    int n = 10;

    int p = (++m) + (++m);  // m=11, m=12, p = 11 + 12 = 23 (undefined for same variable!)
    int q = (n++) + (n++);  // n=10, n=11, q = 10 + 11 = 21 (undefined for same variable!)
    // Note: Multiple modifications to same variable in one expression = undefined behavior

    return 0;
}
```

## IMPLEMENTATION

**How they're implemented for built-in types:**
```cpp
// Prefix (returns reference)
int& operator++(int& x) {
    x = x + 1;
    return x;  // Return reference to modified value
}

// Postfix (returns copy)
int operator++(int& x, int) {  // Dummy int parameter distinguishes postfix
    int temp = x;  // Create copy
    x = x + 1;
    return temp;   // Return old value
}
```

**How to overload for custom classes:**
```cpp
class Counter {
    int value;
public:
    Counter(int v = 0) : value(v) {}

    // Prefix: ++obj
    Counter& operator++() {
        ++value;
        return *this;  // Return reference (no copy)
    }

    // Postfix: obj++
    Counter operator++(int) {  // Dummy int parameter
        Counter temp = *this;  // Save old value (copy)
        ++value;
        return temp;  // Return old value (copy)
    }

    int getValue() const { return value; }
};

Counter c(5);
++c;    // Calls operator++(), returns reference
c++;    // Calls operator++(int), returns copy
```

**Assembly comparison (x86-64):**
```asm
; int i; ++i;
inc DWORD PTR [rbp-4]   ; Direct increment in memory

; int i; int j = i++;
mov eax, DWORD PTR [rbp-4]   ; Load old value into temp register
inc DWORD PTR [rbp-4]        ; Increment in memory
mov DWORD PTR [rbp-8], eax   ; Store old value to j

; For int, compilers optimize away the difference in most cases
```

**Iterator example (where performance matters):**
```cpp
// std::vector<int>::iterator (simplified)
class Iterator {
    int* ptr;
public:
    // Prefix: ++it (efficient)
    Iterator& operator++() {
        ++ptr;          // Just increment pointer
        return *this;   // Return reference (cheap)
    }

    // Postfix: it++ (less efficient)
    Iterator operator++(int) {
        Iterator temp = *this;  // Copy entire iterator object
        ++ptr;
        return temp;  // Return copy (expensive for complex iterators)
    }
};
```

**Performance measurements:**
```cpp
// For built-in int: NO difference (compiler optimizes)
for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) { }   // Same speed
for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; ++i) { }   // Same speed

// For iterators: prefix is faster
for (auto it = vec.begin(); it != vec.end(); it++) { }  // Slower (creates temporary)
for (auto it = vec.begin(); it != vec.end(); ++it) { }  // Faster (no temporary)
```

Typical overhead for postfix with complex types: **5-10% slower** due to copy construction.

**Compiler optimization example:**
```cpp
int i = 0;
i++;  // Compiler optimizes to just: inc i (no return value used)
++i;  // Same assembly generated
```

When return value is **not used**, compilers generate identical code for both.

**Why postfix needs dummy int parameter:**
```cpp
// Without dummy parameter, these would have identical signatures:
Counter& operator++();     // Prefix
Counter operator++();      // ERROR: Can't overload on return type alone!

// Solution: Dummy int parameter distinguishes them
Counter operator++(int);   // Postfix (int is never used, just for overload resolution)
```

**Return type table:**

| Operation | Return type | Returned value | Can chain? |
|-----------|-------------|----------------|------------|
| `++i` | `int&` (reference) | New value | Yes: `++++i` |
| `i++` | `int` (value) | Old value | No: `i++++` is error |
| `++it` | `Iterator&` | Modified iterator | Yes |
| `it++` | `Iterator` (copy) | Old iterator | No |

**Advanced: Chaining prefix (legal but confusing):**
```cpp
int i = 5;
++++i;  // Legal: ((++i)++) - i becomes 7

i++++;  // ERROR: Can't apply ++ to rvalue (temporary)
```

**Best practice summary:**
1. Use `++i` by default (especially in loops)
2. Use `i++` only when you explicitly need the old value
3. Never chain `++` operators
4. For user-defined types, always implement both for consistency
5. Mark postfix `operator++` as `const` friendly if possible

**C vs C++ difference:**
- C doesn't allow overloading, so both are built-in only
- C++ allows custom implementations, making prefix potentially faster
- In C++, range-based for loops use prefix by default: `for (auto& x : vec)`

