}
```

The `factorial` function is `constexpr`.  `fact5` uses it with a compile-time known argument (5), resulting in compile-time evaluation and substitution of 120. `fact6`'s argument `n` is only known at runtime, so the compiler generates code for a runtime function call. The difference in assembly code between the two would be readily apparent: the former would involve no function call, while the latter would show a function call instruction to the factorial function.  The performance impact is evident – the compile-time calculation avoids runtime overhead. Note that the compiler's capacity for compile-time evaluation depends on the complexity of the function. Highly recursive functions or those with conditional logic might not always be fully optimized at compile time, even if declared `constexpr`.

