This example defines a class 'Point' with private data members 'x_' and 'y_', a constructor to initialize these members, and a member function 'display' to output the point's coordinates. In 'main', an object 'p' of type 'Point' is instantiated using the constructor, and its 'display' method is called. This demonstrates how class declaration encapsulates data and behavior, enabling object-oriented design in modern C++.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> numbers = {10, 20, 30, 40};  // A common STL container

    // Using auto in a range-based for loop for iteration
    for (auto& num : numbers) {  // auto deduces 'int&' for modifiable elements
        num *= 2;  // Modify elements in place, a real-world pattern for data processing
        std::cout << num << std::endl;  // Output the modified values
    }

