    std::vector<char> vec = {'H', 'e', 'l', 'l', 'o'};
    std::string str;
    // Using assign with iterator range
    str.assign(vec.begin(), vec.end());
    std::cout << "Assigned from vector: " << str << std::endl;

    // Using assign with initializer_list
    str.assign({'W', 'o', 'r', 'l', 'd'});
    std::cout << "Assigned from initializer_list: " << str << std::endl;

    // Using insert to add a substring at position 5
    str.insert(5, " C++");
    std::cout << "After insert: " << str << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This example demonstrates how the 'assign' member function of std::basic_string can accept different types of input: an iterator range from a vector and an initializer list of characters. It also shows how 'insert' can add a substring at a specific position. These template functions exemplify C++'s ability to write generic, flexible code that works with various data sources, leveraging template parameters to enable type-agnostic operations within the string class.

