## WHAT

**lvalue**: Expression that refers to a memory location (has an address). Can appear on left or right side of assignment. Examples: variables, array elements, dereferenced pointers.
**rvalue**: Temporary expression that doesn't persist beyond the expression. No address. Can only appear on right side of assignment. Examples: literals, temporary objects, return values.

## WHY

- **Understanding lvalues**: Essential for references, pointers, and knowing what can be modified
- **Understanding rvalues**: Critical for move semantics, perfect forwarding, and avoiding unnecessary copies
- **rvalue references (`&&`)**: Enable move semantics - steal resources from temporaries instead of copying
- **Performance**: Moving large objects (vectors, strings) is dramatically faster than copying
- **Perfect forwarding**: `std::forward` preserves value category in template functions

## HOW

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <utility>  // For std::move
using namespace std;

class Resource {
    int* data;
public:
    // Constructor
    Resource(int size) : data(new int[size]) {
        cout << "Constructor\n";
    }

    // Copy constructor (lvalue reference)
    Resource(const Resource& other) : data(new int[100]) {
        cout << "Copy constructor (expensive!)\n";
        // Deep copy...
    }

    // Move constructor (rvalue reference)
    Resource(Resource&& other) noexcept : data(other.data) {
        cout << "Move constructor (cheap!)\n";
        other.data = nullptr;  // Steal resource
    }

    ~Resource() { delete[] data; }
};

Resource createResource() {
    return Resource(100);  // Returns temporary (rvalue)
}

int main() {
    // ========== lvalues ==========
    int x = 5;           // x is lvalue
    int* ptr = &x;       // Can take address of lvalue
    x = 10;              // Can assign to lvalue

    // ========== rvalues ==========
    int y = 42;          // 42 is rvalue (temporary literal)
    // int* ptr2 = &42;  // ERROR: can't take address of rvalue
    // 42 = y;           // ERROR: can't assign to rvalue

    // ========== Move semantics ==========
    Resource r1(100);                    // Constructor
    Resource r2 = r1;                    // Copy (r1 is lvalue)
    Resource r3 = createResource();      // Move (temp is rvalue)
    Resource r4 = std::move(r1);         // Move (std::move converts lvalue to rvalue)

    // ========== Rvalue references ==========
    int&& rref = 42;     // Binds to rvalue
    // int&& bad = x;    // ERROR: can't bind rvalue ref to lvalue

    int& lref = x;       // Binds to lvalue
    // int& bad2 = 42;   // ERROR: can't bind lvalue ref to rvalue (unless const)
    const int& ok = 42;  // OK: const lvalue ref can bind to rvalue

    return 0;
}
```

## IMPLEMENTATION

**Value categories in C++ (simplified):**
```
Expression
├─ lvalue (has identity, can't be moved from)
│   └─ Examples: variables, *ptr, arr[0], ++x
└─ rvalue (no identity, can be moved from)
    ├─ prvalue (pure rvalue): 42, true, nullptr, x + y
    └─ xvalue (expiring value): std::move(x), function returning &&
```

**Full C++11 value category taxonomy:**
```
         expression
        /           \
    glvalue        rvalue
    /     \        /     \
lvalue   xvalue  xvalue  prvalue
```
- **glvalue** (generalized lvalue): has identity (lvalue or xvalue)
- **prvalue** (pure rvalue): doesn't have identity, can be moved from
- **xvalue** (expiring value): has identity but can be moved from

**How the compiler distinguishes:**
```cpp
int x = 5;
int& lref = x;        // Binds to lvalue
int&& rref = 5;       // Binds to rvalue

// Compiler generates different function overloads:
void func(int& x);    // Called for lvalues
void func(int&& x);   // Called for rvalues
```

**std::move internals (it's just a cast!):**
```cpp
template<typename T>
typename std::remove_reference<T>::type&& move(T&& arg) noexcept {
    return static_cast<typename std::remove_reference<T>::type&&>(arg);
}

// std::move(x) is equivalent to:
static_cast<int&&>(x);  // Cast lvalue to rvalue reference
```

**Move constructor vs copy constructor assembly:**
```asm
; Copy constructor (std::vector):
mov rdi, [src]        ; Load source pointer
mov rsi, [src+8]      ; Load source size
call malloc           ; Allocate new memory
; ...copy loop...     ; Copy all elements

; Move constructor (std::vector):
mov rax, [src]        ; Load source pointer
mov [dest], rax       ; Steal pointer (no allocation!)
mov qword [src], 0    ; Null out source
; Done - just 3 instructions vs hundreds for copy!
```

**Performance implications:**
```cpp
// Copy: O(n) time, O(n) space allocation
std::vector<int> v1 = v2;  // Deep copy of all elements

// Move: O(1) time, O(0) space allocation
std::vector<int> v1 = std::move(v2);  // Just copy 3 pointers (data, size, capacity)
```

**Memory layout example (std::vector):**
```
Before move:
v1: [ptr: null] [size: 0] [cap: 0]
v2: [ptr: 0x1000] [size: 1000] [cap: 1000] → heap: [1000 elements]

After std::move(v2):
v1: [ptr: 0x1000] [size: 1000] [cap: 1000] → heap: [1000 elements]
v2: [ptr: null] [size: 0] [cap: 0]  ← moved-from state
```

**Rule of Five (C++11):**
If you implement one of these, implement all five:
1. Destructor
2. Copy constructor
3. Copy assignment operator
4. Move constructor
5. Move assignment operator

**Perfect forwarding:**
```cpp
template<typename T>
void wrapper(T&& arg) {
    // arg is lvalue here (it has a name)
    process(std::forward<T>(arg));  // Preserves original value category
}

int x = 5;
wrapper(x);            // T = int&, forwards as lvalue
wrapper(10);           // T = int, forwards as rvalue
wrapper(std::move(x)); // T = int, forwards as rvalue
```

**Common rvalue reference use cases:**
```cpp
// 1. Move constructor/assignment
class MyClass {
    MyClass(MyClass&& other) noexcept;
    MyClass& operator=(MyClass&& other) noexcept;
};

// 2. Sinking functions (take ownership)
void sink(std::unique_ptr<int>&& ptr);  // Caller must std::move

// 3. Perfect forwarding
template<typename T>
void forward_wrapper(T&& arg) {
    real_function(std::forward<T>(arg));
}
```

**Compiler optimizations:**
- **RVO (Return Value Optimization)**: Eliminates copy/move when returning by value
- **NRVO (Named RVO)**: RVO for named variables
- **Copy elision**: C++17 guarantees elision in some cases

**When NOT to use std::move:**
```cpp
// 1. On return values (prevents RVO)
return std::move(local_var);  // BAD

// 2. On const objects (doesn't move)
return std::move(const_var);  // Just copies

// 3. On trivial types (no benefit)
return std::move(int_var);  // Pointless - int is cheap to copy
```

