**Example:**
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> numbers = {1, 2, 4, 5};
    auto it = numbers.begin() + 2; // iterator pointing to the third element
    numbers.insert(it, 3); // insert '3' before the element pointed by 'it'
    for (const auto& num : numbers) {
        std::cout << num << ' ';
    }
    return 0;
}
```

This example creates a vector of integers and uses an iterator to specify the insertion position. The `insert` function adds the value '3' before the element '4' (which is at position 2). The vector then contains {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}. This demonstrates how `insert` with an iterator position allows precise and efficient insertion of elements into a vector, a core operation in modern C++ container manipulation.

**Syntax:**
Container declaration: std::container_type<element_type> container_name;
Algorithm usage: std::algorithm_name(begin_iterator, end_iterator, optional_parameters);

**Example:**
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> data;
    data.reserve(150000); // Reserve memory upfront for efficiency
    for (int i = 0; i < 150000; ++i) {
        data.push_back(i % 1000); // Insert duplicate elements
    }
    // Remove duplicates using std::unique after sorting
    std::sort(data.begin(), data.end());
    auto last_unique = std::unique(data.begin(), data.end());
    data.erase(last_unique, data.end());
    std::cout << "Unique elements count: " << data.size() << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This example demonstrates the use of std::vector as a dynamic container optimized with reserve() to improve insertion performance. It also shows how to remove duplicate elements efficiently using STL algorithms like std::sort and std::unique, highlighting the flexibility and power of the STL for data processing tasks in C++. The code exemplifies modern C++ practices by leveraging container methods and algorithms to write concise, efficient, and maintainable code.

