In C++, operator overloading allows defining custom behaviors for operators when applied to user-defined types. When combined with templates, it enables generic operator implementations that work across various types, enhancing code reusability and type safety. This is particularly useful for container classes like std::multimap, where comparison operators can be templated to work with different key and value types, ensuring consistent behavior across diverse instantiations.

**Syntax:**
template <class Key, class T, class Compare, class Allocator>
bool operator!=(const multimap<Key,T,Compare,Allocator>& x, const multimap<Key,T,Compare,Allocator>& y);

**Example:**
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <map>

template <typename Key, typename T, typename Compare, typename Allocator>
bool operator!=(const std::multimap<Key, T, Compare, Allocator>& x, const std::multimap<Key, T, Compare, Allocator>& y) {
    return !(x == y);
}

int main() {
    std::multimap<int, std::string> map1 = {{1, "one"}, {2, "two"}};
    std::multimap<int, std::string> map2 = {{1, "one"}, {2, "two"}};

    if (map1 != map2) {
        std::cout << "Maps are different." << std::endl;
    } else {
        std::cout << "Maps are equal." << std::endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```

This example defines a template function that overloads the '!=' operator for std::multimap instances, allowing comparison of two multimaps regardless of their key, value, comparator, or allocator types. In main(), two multimaps are created with identical contents; the overloaded operator is used to compare them, demonstrating how templates facilitate generic operator overloading for standard library containers in modern C++.

## WHAT

**Prefix increment (`++i`)**: Increment first, then return the new value. Returns reference to the incremented variable.
**Postfix increment (`i++`)**: Return current value first, then increment. Returns a copy of the original value (before increment).

## WHY

- **Prefix (`++i`)**: Use when you don't need the old value. **Faster** for complex types (no temporary copy). Preferred in loops.
- **Postfix (`i++`)**: Use only when you explicitly need the value before increment (e.g., `arr[i++]`).
- **Performance**: For built-in types (int, pointer), compilers optimize both equally. For user-defined types (iterators, custom classes), prefix is faster.
- **Best practice**: Default to `++i` in loops, use `i++` only when semantics require it.

## HOW

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int x = 5;
    int y = 5;

    // Prefix: increment first, then use value
    int a = ++x;  // x becomes 6, a = 6
    cout << "Prefix: x = " << x << ", a = " << a << endl;
    // Output: x = 6, a = 6

    // Postfix: use value first, then increment
    int b = y++;  // b = 5 (old value), then y becomes 6
    cout << "Postfix: y = " << y << ", b = " << b << endl;
    // Output: y = 6, b = 5

    // Common loop idiom
    for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) {  // Prefer ++i (no difference for int, but good habit)
        cout << i << " ";
    }
    cout << endl;

    // Using in array indexing
    int arr[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
    int index = 0;

    cout << arr[index++] << endl;  // Prints 10, then index becomes 1
    cout << arr[++index] << endl;  // index becomes 2, prints 30

    // Return value difference
    int m = 10;
    int n = 10;

    int p = (++m) + (++m);  // m=11, m=12, p = 11 + 12 = 23 (undefined for same variable!)
    int q = (n++) + (n++);  // n=10, n=11, q = 10 + 11 = 21 (undefined for same variable!)
    // Note: Multiple modifications to same variable in one expression = undefined behavior

    return 0;
}
```

## IMPLEMENTATION

**How they're implemented for built-in types:**
```cpp
// Prefix (returns reference)
int& operator++(int& x) {
    x = x + 1;
    return x;  // Return reference to modified value
}

// Postfix (returns copy)
int operator++(int& x, int) {  // Dummy int parameter distinguishes postfix
    int temp = x;  // Create copy
    x = x + 1;
    return temp;   // Return old value
}
```

**How to overload for custom classes:**
```cpp
class Counter {
    int value;
public:
    Counter(int v = 0) : value(v) {}

    // Prefix: ++obj
    Counter& operator++() {
        ++value;
        return *this;  // Return reference (no copy)
    }

    // Postfix: obj++
    Counter operator++(int) {  // Dummy int parameter
        Counter temp = *this;  // Save old value (copy)
        ++value;
        return temp;  // Return old value (copy)
    }

    int getValue() const { return value; }
};

Counter c(5);
++c;    // Calls operator++(), returns reference
c++;    // Calls operator++(int), returns copy
```

**Assembly comparison (x86-64):**
```asm
; int i; ++i;
inc DWORD PTR [rbp-4]   ; Direct increment in memory

; int i; int j = i++;
mov eax, DWORD PTR [rbp-4]   ; Load old value into temp register
inc DWORD PTR [rbp-4]        ; Increment in memory
mov DWORD PTR [rbp-8], eax   ; Store old value to j

; For int, compilers optimize away the difference in most cases
```

**Iterator example (where performance matters):**
```cpp
// std::vector<int>::iterator (simplified)
class Iterator {
    int* ptr;
public:
    // Prefix: ++it (efficient)
    Iterator& operator++() {
        ++ptr;          // Just increment pointer
        return *this;   // Return reference (cheap)
    }

    // Postfix: it++ (less efficient)
    Iterator operator++(int) {
        Iterator temp = *this;  // Copy entire iterator object
        ++ptr;
        return temp;  // Return copy (expensive for complex iterators)
    }
};
```

**Performance measurements:**
```cpp
// For built-in int: NO difference (compiler optimizes)
for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) { }   // Same speed
for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; ++i) { }   // Same speed

// For iterators: prefix is faster
for (auto it = vec.begin(); it != vec.end(); it++) { }  // Slower (creates temporary)
for (auto it = vec.begin(); it != vec.end(); ++it) { }  // Faster (no temporary)
```

Typical overhead for postfix with complex types: **5-10% slower** due to copy construction.

**Compiler optimization example:**
```cpp
int i = 0;
i++;  // Compiler optimizes to just: inc i (no return value used)
++i;  // Same assembly generated
```

When return value is **not used**, compilers generate identical code for both.

**Why postfix needs dummy int parameter:**
```cpp
// Without dummy parameter, these would have identical signatures:
Counter& operator++();     // Prefix
Counter operator++();      // ERROR: Can't overload on return type alone!

// Solution: Dummy int parameter distinguishes them
Counter operator++(int);   // Postfix (int is never used, just for overload resolution)
```

**Return type table:**

| Operation | Return type | Returned value | Can chain? |
|-----------|-------------|----------------|------------|
| `++i` | `int&` (reference) | New value | Yes: `++++i` |
| `i++` | `int` (value) | Old value | No: `i++++` is error |
| `++it` | `Iterator&` | Modified iterator | Yes |
| `it++` | `Iterator` (copy) | Old iterator | No |

**Advanced: Chaining prefix (legal but confusing):**
```cpp
int i = 5;
++++i;  // Legal: ((++i)++) - i becomes 7

i++++;  // ERROR: Can't apply ++ to rvalue (temporary)
```

**Best practice summary:**
1. Use `++i` by default (especially in loops)
2. Use `i++` only when you explicitly need the old value
3. Never chain `++` operators
4. For user-defined types, always implement both for consistency
5. Mark postfix `operator++` as `const` friendly if possible

**C vs C++ difference:**
- C doesn't allow overloading, so both are built-in only
- C++ allows custom implementations, making prefix potentially faster
- In C++, range-based for loops use prefix by default: `for (auto& x : vec)`

```cpp
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>
int shared_var = 0;
void *thread_func(void *arg) {
    shared_var++;  // Intended to be atomic but may be interrupted
    return NULL;
}
int main() {
    pthread_t thread1, thread2;
    pthread_create(&thread1, NULL, thread_func, NULL);
    pthread_create(&thread2, NULL, thread_func, NULL);
    pthread_join(thread1, NULL);
    pthread_join(thread2, NULL);
    printf("Shared var: %d\n", shared_var);  // May output 1 or 2, demonstrating potential violation
    return 0;
}
```

This example creates two threads that each increment a shared variable without synchronization. The increment operation is not atomic, so an atomicity violation may occur if one thread interrupts the other, potentially resulting in an incorrect final value for shared_var, thus illustrating how such violations lead to unpredictable behavior in concurrent programs.

## WHAT

**`const int*`** (pointer to const int): Pointer can change, but the pointed-to value cannot be modified through this pointer.
**`int* const`** (const pointer to int): Pointer cannot change (always points to same address), but the pointed-to value can be modified.
**`const int* const`** (const pointer to const int): Neither the pointer nor the pointed-to value can be changed.

## WHY

- **`const int*`**: Use when passing data to functions that should read but not modify it (e.g., `strlen(const char* str)`). Protects data from accidental modification.
- **`int* const`**: Use when a pointer must always point to the same location but the value at that location needs to be mutable (e.g., hardware registers, fixed buffer addresses).
- **`const int* const`**: Use when both the pointer location AND the data are immutable (rare, mostly in read-only configuration tables or security-critical code).

## HOW

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int x = 10, y = 20;

    // Pointer to const int
    const int* ptr1 = &x;
    // *ptr1 = 15;     // ❌ ERROR: can't modify value
    ptr1 = &y;         // ✅ OK: can change pointer

    // Const pointer to int
    int* const ptr2 = &x;
    *ptr2 = 15;        // ✅ OK: can modify value
    // ptr2 = &y;      // ❌ ERROR: can't change pointer

    // Const pointer to const int
    const int* const ptr3 = &x;
    // *ptr3 = 15;     // ❌ ERROR: can't modify value
    // ptr3 = &y;      // ❌ ERROR: can't change pointer

    printf("x = %d, y = %d\n", x, y);
    return 0;
}
```

## IMPLEMENTATION

**Compiler behavior:**
- `const` is enforced at **compile-time** only - no runtime overhead
- Compiler performs type checking to prevent writes through `const int*`
- At runtime, ALL three variations are identical in memory (just a pointer address)
- `const` can be cast away with `(int*)`, but this is undefined behavior if the original object was truly const

**Memory layout:**
All three are identical - a single pointer (8 bytes on 64-bit):
```
const int* ptr;       → [8-byte address]
int* const ptr;       → [8-byte address]
const int* const ptr; → [8-byte address]
```

**Type checking examples:**
```c
void read_only(const int* p) { /* can't modify *p */ }
void mutable_func(int* p) { /* can modify *p */ }

int x = 5;
const int y = 10;

read_only(&x);  // ✅ OK: implicit conversion to const
read_only(&y);  // ✅ OK: y is already const

mutable_func(&x);  // ✅ OK: x is non-const
// mutable_func(&y);  // ❌ ERROR: discards const qualifier
```

**Assembly implications:**
The compiler generates identical assembly for all three - the difference is purely in type safety at compile-time:
```asm
; All three compile to same thing:
mov rax, [address_of_x]   ; Load pointer value
mov eax, [rax]            ; Dereference (if allowed by const)
```

**Performance:**
- Zero runtime cost - `const` is a compile-time constraint
- Can enable optimizations: compiler knows `const int*` won't modify data, allowing aggressive caching in registers
- Example: `sum(const int* arr, int n)` - compiler can assume `arr` contents don't change during function execution

## WHAT

**lvalue**: Expression that refers to a memory location (has an address). Can appear on left or right side of assignment. Examples: variables, array elements, dereferenced pointers.
**rvalue**: Temporary expression that doesn't persist beyond the expression. No address. Can only appear on right side of assignment. Examples: literals, temporary objects, return values.

## WHY

- **Understanding lvalues**: Essential for references, pointers, and knowing what can be modified
- **Understanding rvalues**: Critical for move semantics, perfect forwarding, and avoiding unnecessary copies
- **rvalue references (`&&`)**: Enable move semantics - steal resources from temporaries instead of copying
- **Performance**: Moving large objects (vectors, strings) is dramatically faster than copying
- **Perfect forwarding**: `std::forward` preserves value category in template functions

## HOW

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <utility>  // For std::move
using namespace std;

class Resource {
    int* data;
public:
    // Constructor
    Resource(int size) : data(new int[size]) {
        cout << "Constructor\n";
    }

    // Copy constructor (lvalue reference)
    Resource(const Resource& other) : data(new int[100]) {
        cout << "Copy constructor (expensive!)\n";
        // Deep copy...
    }

    // Move constructor (rvalue reference)
    Resource(Resource&& other) noexcept : data(other.data) {
        cout << "Move constructor (cheap!)\n";
        other.data = nullptr;  // Steal resource
    }

    ~Resource() { delete[] data; }
};

Resource createResource() {
    return Resource(100);  // Returns temporary (rvalue)
}

int main() {
    // ========== lvalues ==========
    int x = 5;           // x is lvalue
    int* ptr = &x;       // Can take address of lvalue
    x = 10;              // Can assign to lvalue

    // ========== rvalues ==========
    int y = 42;          // 42 is rvalue (temporary literal)
    // int* ptr2 = &42;  // ERROR: can't take address of rvalue
    // 42 = y;           // ERROR: can't assign to rvalue

    // ========== Move semantics ==========
    Resource r1(100);                    // Constructor
    Resource r2 = r1;                    // Copy (r1 is lvalue)
    Resource r3 = createResource();      // Move (temp is rvalue)
    Resource r4 = std::move(r1);         // Move (std::move converts lvalue to rvalue)

    // ========== Rvalue references ==========
    int&& rref = 42;     // Binds to rvalue
    // int&& bad = x;    // ERROR: can't bind rvalue ref to lvalue

    int& lref = x;       // Binds to lvalue
    // int& bad2 = 42;   // ERROR: can't bind lvalue ref to rvalue (unless const)
    const int& ok = 42;  // OK: const lvalue ref can bind to rvalue

    return 0;
}
```

## IMPLEMENTATION

**Value categories in C++ (simplified):**
```
Expression
├─ lvalue (has identity, can't be moved from)
│   └─ Examples: variables, *ptr, arr[0], ++x
└─ rvalue (no identity, can be moved from)
    ├─ prvalue (pure rvalue): 42, true, nullptr, x + y
    └─ xvalue (expiring value): std::move(x), function returning &&
```

**Full C++11 value category taxonomy:**
```
         expression
        /           \
    glvalue        rvalue
    /     \        /     \
lvalue   xvalue  xvalue  prvalue
```
- **glvalue** (generalized lvalue): has identity (lvalue or xvalue)
- **prvalue** (pure rvalue): doesn't have identity, can be moved from
- **xvalue** (expiring value): has identity but can be moved from

**How the compiler distinguishes:**
```cpp
int x = 5;
int& lref = x;        // Binds to lvalue
int&& rref = 5;       // Binds to rvalue

// Compiler generates different function overloads:
void func(int& x);    // Called for lvalues
void func(int&& x);   // Called for rvalues
```

**std::move internals (it's just a cast!):**
```cpp
template<typename T>
typename std::remove_reference<T>::type&& move(T&& arg) noexcept {
    return static_cast<typename std::remove_reference<T>::type&&>(arg);
}

// std::move(x) is equivalent to:
static_cast<int&&>(x);  // Cast lvalue to rvalue reference
```

**Move constructor vs copy constructor assembly:**
```asm
; Copy constructor (std::vector):
mov rdi, [src]        ; Load source pointer
mov rsi, [src+8]      ; Load source size
call malloc           ; Allocate new memory
; ...copy loop...     ; Copy all elements

; Move constructor (std::vector):
mov rax, [src]        ; Load source pointer
mov [dest], rax       ; Steal pointer (no allocation!)
mov qword [src], 0    ; Null out source
; Done - just 3 instructions vs hundreds for copy!
```

**Performance implications:**
```cpp
// Copy: O(n) time, O(n) space allocation
std::vector<int> v1 = v2;  // Deep copy of all elements

// Move: O(1) time, O(0) space allocation
std::vector<int> v1 = std::move(v2);  // Just copy 3 pointers (data, size, capacity)
```

**Memory layout example (std::vector):**
```
Before move:
v1: [ptr: null] [size: 0] [cap: 0]
v2: [ptr: 0x1000] [size: 1000] [cap: 1000] → heap: [1000 elements]

After std::move(v2):
v1: [ptr: 0x1000] [size: 1000] [cap: 1000] → heap: [1000 elements]
v2: [ptr: null] [size: 0] [cap: 0]  ← moved-from state
```

**Rule of Five (C++11):**
If you implement one of these, implement all five:
1. Destructor
2. Copy constructor
3. Copy assignment operator
4. Move constructor
5. Move assignment operator

**Perfect forwarding:**
```cpp
template<typename T>
void wrapper(T&& arg) {
    // arg is lvalue here (it has a name)
    process(std::forward<T>(arg));  // Preserves original value category
}

int x = 5;
wrapper(x);            // T = int&, forwards as lvalue
wrapper(10);           // T = int, forwards as rvalue
wrapper(std::move(x)); // T = int, forwards as rvalue
```

**Common rvalue reference use cases:**
```cpp
// 1. Move constructor/assignment
