  D d;
  std::cout << &d.a << " \n"; // Address of A will not be directly embedded
  //Observe the address of a, and its difference from b and c to understand the layout
  std::cout << &d.b << " \n";
  std::cout << &d.c << " \n";
  std::cout << &d.d << " \n";
  return 0;
}
```

This example demonstrates virtual inheritance. The output will show that the addresses of `d.a`, `d.b`, `d.c`, and `d.d` are likely to be different.  The address of `a` will likely not be directly embedded in `D`, but accessed indirectly through a pointer (or offset) to the shared virtual base class `A`.  Examining the memory addresses reveals the compiler's strategy for managing the shared virtual base class and the overall object layout. Disassembling the compiled code can further illustrate the pointer manipulation and indirect memory access involved in accessing member `a`.  The differences in addresses and the assembly instructions used highlight the implementation details of virtual inheritance and the virtual base class mechanism.  Different compilers will show slightly different memory layouts and addressing schemes depending on their optimization and platform specific details.

